# A contributor's own presets. CMake reads this file alongside
# CMakePresets.json, which is the shared source of truth and is checked
# in; this one is where personal paths, generators and toolchains go, and
# committing it would put them in everyone's build. Anchored to the root
# for the same reason /CLAUDE.local.md is: a copy in a subdirectory is
# shared drift rather than personal, and should stay visible.
/CMakeUserPresets.json

# Saved games. freeze opens the name it was given without qualifying it
# (setup.c:55), so an ordinary one lands in whatever directory the game
# is playing in -- and that is the source tree: AGENTS.md's "Build and
# run" says ./build/debug/sst from the root, and the in-game help reads
# sst.doc from the current directory (sst.c:161), so playing anywhere
# else is awkward. The emergency exit, emexit, freezes to a fixed
# emsave.trk the same way (sst.c:356, setup.c:38-39). Unanchored,
# unlike the two rules around it, because the game plays in whatever
# directory it was started from, and that need not be the root.
#
# The rule keys on the extension, so what it catches follows from what
# freeze does to the name you type: scan() lowercases it and keeps the
# first nine characters (sst.c:788), then .trk is appended only if what
# survives has no dot (setup.c:51-53). So `freeze mygame` is caught, and
# so is a typed `freeze save.trk`; `freeze save.dat` is not, and neither
# is `freeze mysave.trk`, which truncates to mysave.tr -- dot intact --
# before the extension is ever considered. The plaque at the end of a
# game gets none of that shaping: it goes through readinput rather than
# scan, so it opens the name as typed, with neither the nine characters
# nor the extension applied to it (finish.c:422-425).
*.trk

# Personal, unshared agent instructions -- shared rules go in AGENTS.md.
# Anchored to the root, matching the only path tests/instructions.sh
# exempts; a CLAUDE.local.md in a subdirectory is shared drift, not
# personal, and should stay visible.
/CLAUDE.local.md
